{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R1 — TCP wire framing: every frame is a 4-byte big-endian length followed by that many bytes",
    "of CBOR payload.",
    "",
    "Batch 2 (`connectivity`). Reached because `connectivity/tcp_connect` cites `§8`, and §8 in this",
    "snapshot is Constants and says nothing about TCP. The text lives at §1.6. See FINDINGS F9."
  ],
  "id": "ECP-R1",
  "id_status": "allocated",
  "title": "Over TCP, the peer reads and writes frames as a 4-byte big-endian length prefix followed by exactly that many bytes of CBOR",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §1.6",
  "spec_also": [
    "§4.1 (the first exchange on a connection is hello)",
    "§4.10(a) (the finite inbound bound the control relies on)",
    "§9.1 (the floor row this id binds — which also covers §1.6's WebSocket rules, see ECP-R1-pending-b/c)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F9",
  "reading": [
    "§1.6: \"Frame := [4 bytes: length (big-endian)] [length bytes: CBOR payload]. This defines the default",
    "TCP framing.\"",
    "",
    "THE LEVEL IS THE FLOOR ROW'S, NOT A KEYWORD'S. §1.6's TCP paragraph carries no RFC 2119 keyword: it",
    "DEFINES the frame. The MUST comes from §9.1, whose first row is \"Wire framing (§1.6)\". A definition",
    "placed on the floor is an obligation to use it; there is no conformant alternative TCP framing,",
    "because §1.6 says other framings belong to OTHER transports (\"Other transports (QUIC, shared",
    "memory, etc.) define their own framing and limits.\"). Recorded because a reader looking for \"MUST\" in §1.6's TCP text will not find one,",
    "and that is not a gap — see F11 for where the same shape is a gap.",
    "",
    "WHAT IS OBSERVABLE. Both directions:",
    "  (i)  the peer PARSES the prefix as big-endian and reads exactly that many bytes -- observed by the",
    "       peer answering a hello sent this way;",
    "  (ii) the peer WRITES frames this way -- observed by the response's first 4 bytes, read big-endian,",
    "       equalling the byte length of the CBOR item that follows, with nothing left over before the",
    "       next frame.",
    "A check asserting only \"the TCP connection was accepted\" measures the operating system's listen",
    "socket, not §1.6. A check asserting only that a hello was answered measures (i) and not (ii).",
    "",
    "NOT ASSERTED: the SHOULD \"TCP implementations SHOULD use a reasonable default frame limit (e.g.,",
    "16 MiB)\". It is §4.10(a)'s territory, where the finite bound is a MUST in its own right (ECP-R66,",
    "ECP-R66-pending-d), and a second requirement on the same bound at a weaker level would score one",
    "behaviour twice.",
    "",
    "NOT ASSERTED: the ~4 GiB ceiling. It is arithmetic (\"supports frames up to\"), not an obligation."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "transport"
    ],
    "note": "transport = tcp. The address the peer listens on is part of the run's posture."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "framed-hello-answered-framed",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a fresh TCP connection; a hello EXECUTE framed per §1.6",
      "why": "§1.6 in both directions, via the first exchange §4.1 requires.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "tcp_open"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_raw_frame",
          "capture": "hello",
          "payload": "a well-formed hello envelope (§4.1, §3.8)",
          "prefix": "u32 big-endian, = len(payload)"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "frame_prefix_matches_payload",
          "capture": "hello",
          "why": "(ii): the first 4 response bytes, big-endian, equal the length of exactly one CBOR data item that follows. Checked on the bytes, before any decode — a lenient decoder that tolerates trailing bytes would hide a wrong prefix."
        },
        {
          "kind": "decodes_as_envelope",
          "capture": "hello",
          "why": "(i) is shown by an answer existing at all; that the answer is an EXECUTE_RESPONSE envelope shows the peer read our frame as one hello and not as a fragment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "a fresh TCP connection; the SAME hello bytes with the length prefix written LITTLE-endian",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY. Proves the conformant arm's answer depended on the peer reading the prefix BIG-endian, not on a peer that sniffs both byte orders or ignores the prefix and parses CBOR greedily. For a hello of a few hundred bytes the little-endian prefix read big-endian is > 2^24 bytes, so a big-endian reader waits for, or refuses, an enormous frame — and §4.10(a) permits either. The assertion is only that the hello is NOT answered as a hello.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "tcp_open"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_raw_frame",
          "capture": "le_hello",
          "payload": "the identical hello payload",
          "prefix": "u32 little-endian, = len(payload)",
          "note": "the payload length MUST be chosen so the two byte orders differ and the big-endian reading exceeds 16 MiB; a palindromic length makes this control vacuous."
        }
      ],
      "accept": [
        {
          "outcome": "no_response_within_bound",
          "why": "a big-endian reader is waiting for the rest of a very large frame."
        },
        {
          "outcome": "close",
          "why": "a big-endian reader refused an over-bound frame (§4.10(a) permits a close)."
        },
        {
          "outcome": "coded_response",
          "status": 413,
          "code": "payload_too_large",
          "why": "§4.10(a)'s coded refusal."
        }
      ],
      "refuse": [
        {
          "outcome": "hello_answered",
          "why": "the peer did not use the big-endian prefix to delimit the frame."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer that delimits inbound messages by attempting a CBOR decode on the stream, ignoring the prefix",
      "expect": "answers both the big-endian and the little-endian hello",
      "why": "the shape the control separates from a conformant peer. It interoperates with every client that frames correctly, which is why nothing but a control like this one finds it."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "connectivity/tcp_connect",
    "oracle_note": "provenance only. Its citation `§8` does not resolve to TCP text in this snapshot (§8 is Constants); this file is authored from §1.6. F9."
  }
}
